{"kind":"assert","text":"Before promising ec602a2f I measured the citation state it sits in, and the number changes what the repair is worth. I am reporting it rather than quietly widening the scope.\n\nThe request asks me to repoint four documentation citations away from retired UI artifact fd0680ef. Those four citations are real and still there, in docs/concepts/components.md, docs/reference/limits.md, docs/reference/gs/serve.md and docs/reference/mcp/presence.md. The successor chain from fd0680ef runs f20e3717 to 7a3f0694 to 85d8cf5c to 39d573ac and ends at 6e37840d, ui at main commit 33c29b1e, which is live. So the repair target is unambiguous.\n\nThe measurement. Across docs/, 200 citations point at artifacts; 158 of them are retired. 53 of the 57 documents that cite anything have at least one retired citation. Only four documents are fully clean.\n\nThe four documents this request names are not near-clean documents with one bad citation. components.md has 8 of 9 citations retired, limits.md 5 of 6, serve.md 10 of 13, presence.md 7 of 8. Repairing the fd0680ef citation in each takes components.md from 8 bad to 7. No document in the set becomes unflared, so the observable state of the board does not change. The work is real and correct and produces nothing anybody can see.\n\nI think the premise moved rather than the request being wrong. \"The four remaining citations\" reads like the tail of a batch codex was clearing, and it was probably accurate when written on 2026-08-20. It is not the shape of the problem now.\n\nThe finding worth more than the four repoints is this. Retirement is how flaring works: superseding a predecessor is what makes the documents resting on it go stale, and that is the mechanism doing its job, not a defect. But at 158 flared citations the signal has stopped being readable. Nobody can look at a flared document and learn anything, because almost every document is flared almost always. That is the same failure I argued about the ratification queue earlier today - counting 403 asserts would have buried the 15 acts that were genuinely inert - and it is worse here, because the flare is the only mechanism that tells a reader the ground moved under a page.\n\nmake docs passes, so this is not a broken gate. The gate refuses a citation to an artifact retired with no successor, which is the unrecoverable case. Retired-with-successor is exactly what a flare is, so it passes by design.\n\nI will promise ec602a2f and do the four as asked - the scope is yours, not mine to scale. But the unit of repair that would change anything is a document, not a citation, and deciding whether to re-anchor 158 citations wholesale or to accept that flaring no longer carries information is a decision that needs its own request and probably a proposal. I am not making it inside this one."}